---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-hyperparathyroidism`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Hyperparathyroidism',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Hyperparathyroidism', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Hyperparathyroidism: Primary vs Secondary, PTH Suppression, Cautious Repletion"
  description="Primary and secondary hyperparathyroidism — how vitamin D repletion helps secondary (deficiency-driven) PTH elevation and how to correct deficiency safely in primary HPT."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-hyperparathyroidism", label: "Hyperparathyroidism" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Hyperparathyroidism</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Parathyroid hormone (PTH) rises when calcium falls, keeping serum calcium in
      the tight physiological range. Vitamin D deficiency lowers calcium absorption,
      which raises PTH (secondary hyperparathyroidism). Autonomous parathyroid
      overactivity (primary hyperparathyroidism) raises PTH and calcium simultaneously.
      Vitamin D dosing differs sharply between the two.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Secondary hyperparathyroidism — vitamin D deficiency driven</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      The most common cause of elevated PTH. Vitamin D deficiency → reduced
      intestinal calcium absorption → tendency to hypocalcaemia → parathyroid
      responds by raising PTH → PTH mobilises calcium from bone. Chronic pattern
      accelerates bone loss and increases fracture risk. Correcting vitamin D
      deficiency normalises PTH over weeks to months, protecting bone.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Primary hyperparathyroidism — autonomous</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Adenoma or hyperplasia of one or more parathyroid glands produces excess
      PTH independent of calcium feedback. Serum calcium is high, PTH is
      inappropriately high or normal. Osteoporosis, kidney stones, and
      neurocognitive symptoms result. Definitive treatment is parathyroidectomy
      in symptomatic disease.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Vitamin D deficiency in primary HPT</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Many primary HPT patients have concomitant vitamin D deficiency (partly from
      chronic urinary calcium losses). The traditional concern was that vitamin D
      supplementation would worsen hypercalcaemia. Recent evidence (Rolighed 2014)
      shows cautious vitamin D repletion in primary HPT is safe when calcium is
      monitored — 400–1,000 IU/day maintenance is standard, higher for correction
      with monitoring.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Tertiary hyperparathyroidism</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Long-standing secondary HPT (usually in CKD) leads to autonomous parathyroid
      overactivity even after the underlying cause is corrected. Treatment options
      include cinacalcet, active vitamin D analogues, or parathyroidectomy under
      specialist care.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical guide</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Persistently elevated PTH: check serum calcium, 25(OH)D, and renal function.</li>
      <li>Low 25(OH)D + high PTH + normal or low calcium → secondary HPT from deficiency. Correct vitamin D; re-check PTH at 8–12 weeks.</li>
      <li>High calcium + high PTH → primary HPT. Endocrine referral for imaging (sestamibi scan, ultrasound), consideration of surgery.</li>
      <li>Vitamin D correction in primary HPT is generally safe with calcium monitoring; work with endocrinology.</li>
    </ol>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Elevated PTH always deserves an
      etiological workup — differentiating primary from secondary changes
      treatment fundamentally.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-hyperparathyroidism" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
